y all heard ed's  talk about where we're going to get  protein in the future and if that  happens  that's going to serve to decrease the  value of maize protein  freeing up freeing up  nitrogen for recycling on field  so one of the things that i would like  to do this year and i will be doing this  year is some engineering modeling and  economic modeling  to help us answer this question of where  of whether nitrogen  and when nitrogen will become available  for reuse  so moving on to physiology physiology  and the genetics real quick  i just want to point out that maize is  really well positioned to allow for two  product streams  kernels and cobs they're manageable size  and mass  and i also want to point out that the  mace cobb is mostly vascular  and structural tissue and  this storage of nutrients and vascular  and structural tissues is not new and so  the physiology of storing  nitrogen and phosphorus within the maize  cod is not something that's incredibly  outlandish this is  these are bark storage proteins and  populous and so  in the winter we can see that there's a  lot of protein storage vacuoles  and then in the summer those all  disappear because they've been removed  all that protein has been re-mobilized  for leaves for photosynthesis  focusing even with an even  with an even uh  even closer closer up image of these  protein storage vacuoles  we see that phosphorus is stored  in a very similar manner in these  perennial species as it is in  the maze kernel and so this begs the  question of  are protein storage and phosphorus  storage really evolutionarily  conserved and is it just the location of  the deposition  nitrogen and phosphorus would be  deposited in seeds whereas in perennials  it's stored in permeating tissues so  that it can be used year after year and  so  that makes the question is it  evolutionary con  evolutionarily conservative and can we  use that  to uh to develop a uh  a maze a maze plant that stores  nutrients in the cavity of the